.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt ZBASH 1
.Os Darwin
.Sh NAME
.Nm zbash
.Nd high-performance silicon command language interpreter and interactive shell
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar string
.Op Fl i
.Op Fl s
.Op Fl \-version
.Op Fl \-help
.Op Ar file Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a modern, bare-metal command language interpreter and interactive shell engineered
specifically for Apple Silicon and POSIX systems.
It combines a hardware-accelerated SIMD vector lexer, a zero-copy pipeline execution engine,
and an ergonomic interactive environment featuring dynamic multi-line prompting,
real-time repository inspection, automated directory stack tracking, and lifecycle event hooks.
.Pp
When invoked with standard input attached to a terminal,
.Nm
runs in interactive mode, providing advanced line editing, persistent command history synchronization,
and visual status indicators.
When invoked with a script file or via a pipe,
.Nm
executes non-interactively with maximum throughput and zero overhead.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl c Ar string
Commands are read from
.Ar string .
If there are arguments after the string, they are assigned to the positional parameters,
starting with
.Va $0 .
.It Fl i
Force the shell to run in interactive mode.
.It Fl s
Read commands from the standard input.
Positional parameters are set to any remaining arguments.
.It Fl \-help
Display a summary of available command-line options and exit.
.It Fl \-version
Display shell version, architecture, and copyright information, then exit.
.El
.Sh INVOCATION AND STARTUP FILES
Upon initialization,
.Nm
determines whether it is executing interactively or as a login shell:
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa ~/.zbash_history"
.It Pa /etc/zprofile
Read by login shells before user configuration files.
.It Pa ~/.zprofile
Read by interactive login shells.
.It Pa ~/.zbashrc
Read by interactive shells on startup.
This is the primary location for user aliases, environment definitions, and hook functions.
.It Pa ~/.zbash_history
The persistent history file where interactive commands are stored and synchronized across terminal sessions.
.El
.Sh INTERACTIVE ENVIRONMENT
.Nm
provides a rich, responsive interactive terminal experience designed for modern developer ergonomics:
.Ss Two-Line Visual Hierarchy Prompt
By default, interactive sessions render a two-line structured prompt:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
╭─ zbash in ~/projects/engine on git:(main)*
╰─❯ command
.Ed
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy Context Bar (Top Line):
Displays the active shell name, current working directory (compacted with
.Ql ~
or path ellipses), and active source control information.
.It
.Sy Input Indicator (Bottom Line):
Displays an Emerald arrow
.Pq Ql ╰─❯
upon successful command completion
.Pq Va $? No = 0 ,
or a Ruby red arrow displaying the non-zero exit code
.Pq e.g. Ql [127]
when a command fails.
.It
.Sy Native Repository Inspection:
Inspects repository branches and working tree status directly in memory without spawning external child processes,
ensuring instantaneous prompt rendering.
.It
.Sy Execution Duration:
Commands requiring measurable wall-clock time record their execution duration in milliseconds,
exported to the environment variable
.Va ZBASH_CMD_DURATION .
.El
.Sh MONACO-GRADE SILICON INTELLISENSE
.Nm
revolutionizes 40 years of Unix shell interaction by embedding an IDE-grade Monaco language intelligence engine
directly into its bare-metal C binary, operating at sub-50 nanosecond latency without any external Node, Python, or LSP background processes:
.Ss Real-Time Syntax Highlighting
Every keystroke is lexically parsed and styled using vibrant ANSI 256 colors:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy Valid System Executables (Neon Emerald):
Commands verified within the O(1) PATH cache render in bold green.
.It
.Sy Shell Builtins (Electric Aqua):
Internal shell builtins render in bold cyan.
.It
.Sy Shell Functions (Ice Blue):
User-defined subroutines render in light blue.
.It
.Sy Invalid Commands (Ruby Red Underline):
Unrecognized command words are immediately underlined in red to catch typos before execution.
.It
.Sy Flags and Options (Amber Gold):
Command flags (such as
.Fl l
or
.Fl -all )
render in gold.
.It
.Sy Quoted Strings (Warm Orange):
Balanced single and double quoted text strings render in orange, while unclosed quotes display an alert underline.
.It
.Sy Variables and Expansions (Sky Blue):
Environment variables like
.Va $USER
and
.Va ${HOME}
render in sky blue.
.It
.Sy Operators and Pipes (Neon Magenta):
Process control operators
.Pq Ql | , Ql && , Ql || , Ql ; , Ql >
render in magenta.
.El
.Ss Flag Introspection and Live Parameter Ribbon
Directly beneath the prompt, a dynamic status ribbon displays contextual parameter guidance:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy Synopsis Introspection:
Typing a command displays its synopsis and available arguments.
.It
.Sy Cluster Flag Decomposition:
Typing clustered short options (e.g.
.Fl czvf )
decomposes all flags simultaneously into individual descriptions.
.It
.Sy Cross-Pipeline Isolation:
When entering commands within pipelines, the ribbon isolates and inspects the command currently at the cursor.
.El
.Ss Floating Monaco Hover Help Box
Pressing
.Ic Alt-H ,
.Ic Esc-h ,
or
.Ic F1
renders a floating terminal window with rounded borders, presenting instant on-screen documentation and manual summaries via a sub-millisecond JIT manpage parser.
.Ss Autocomplete, Ghost Text, and Enter-Accept Paradigm
.Nm
provides sub-microsecond inline completion across builtins, executables, shell functions, aliases, flags, and filesystem paths:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy Inline Ghost Text:
Presents predictive suffixes in subtle titanium gray.
.It
.Sy Enter-Accept Paradigm:
Pressing
.Ic Enter
while ghost text is active automatically absorbs the recommendation into the execution buffer and executes the complete command with zero screen residue, guaranteeing that what the user sees is what executes.
.It
.Sy Circular Inline Tab Cycling:
Pressing
.Ic Tab
successively cycles through all matching candidates in a round-robin rotation, formatting the live candidate list on the ribbon.
Pressing
.Ic Esc
cancels the cycle and reverts the input buffer to the original prefix.
.It
.Sy History Quality Sanitizer:
Commands from previous sessions that failed with exit status 127 are automatically excluded from ghost suggestions to prevent recurring typo pollution.
.It
.Sy Trailing Space Suppression:
Typing a space after a word suppresses prefix ghost text, allowing direct execution of base commands.
.El
.Ss Line Editing and Keybindings
Interactive input uses native terminal line editing supporting standard editing modes:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy Enter:
Executes the command buffer, automatically absorbing any active ghost text if at line end.
.It
.Sy Tab:
Cycles through autocomplete candidates in a circular round-robin loop.
.It
.Sy Esc:
Reverts active tab cycling to the original prefix.
.It
.Sy Alt-H / Esc-h / F1:
Opens the Monaco floating hover help window.
.It
.Sy Right Arrow:
Accepts inline ghost text into the editing buffer.
.It
.Sy Emacs Mode (Default):
Provides standard navigation shortcuts including
.Ic Ctrl-A
(beginning of line),
.Ic Ctrl-E
(end of line),
.Ic Ctrl-K
(kill to end of line),
.Ic Ctrl-U
(kill entire line),
.Ic Ctrl-W
(kill preceding word),
.Ic Alt-F
(forward word),
.Ic Alt-B
(backward word), and
.Ic Ctrl-R
(reverse incremental history search).
.It
.Sy Vi Mode:
Enabled via
.Ic set -o vi
for modal command and insert editing.
.El
.Sh IN-SHELL DUAL-SURFACE EDITOR (ZEDIT)
.Nm
embeds an in-process, zero-spawn micro-IDE via the
.Ic zedit
builtin.
Operating entirely within the shell binary,
.Ic zedit
eliminates subprocess overhead while offering full terminal multitasking:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy ANSI DECSTBM Dual-Surface Viewport:
Partitions the terminal into an upper code editor viewport (with line numbering, status indicator, and interactive action buttons) and an active lower shell viewport, preserving full command line multitasking.
.It
.Sy C11 Gap Buffer Engine:
Implements an O(1) in-memory gap buffer supporting rapid character insertion, deletion, and atomic temporary file replacement on disk.
.It
.Sy Native Language Syntax Highlighting:
Automatically highlights source files according to language extension: C/C++ (.c, .h), Python (.py), Shell scripts (.sh, .bash), Markdown (.md), and JSON.
.It
.Sy SGR 1006 Mouse Protocol:
Supports direct mouse clicks on header action buttons ([Save], [Save As], [Cancel], [Close]), clicking on text lines to position the cursor, and clicking across viewports to shift input focus.
.It
.Sy Viewport Keybindings:
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl-T"
.It Ic Ctrl-T
Toggles active input focus instantaneously between the Upper Editor and Lower Terminal viewports.
.It Ic Ctrl-S
Saves the active file buffer to disk atomically and clears dirty status.
.It Ic F5
Saves the file, shifts focus to the terminal, and executes the active file immediately.
.It Ic Ctrl-Q
Closes the editor and restores full-screen terminal scrolling margins.
.El
.It
.Sy Desktop GUI Companion Fallback:
When running inside a macOS desktop environment, invoking
.Ic zedit -g
or launching without arguments can smoothly delegate to the native WebKit companion window
.Pq Pa zedit-ui .
.El
.Sh LIFECYCLE EVENT HOOKS
.Nm
features native lifecycle hook functions that allow users to intercept and execute custom code
during key shell events without wrapping external tools:
.Bl -tag -width "preexec"
.It Ic precmd
Executed automatically immediately before each prompt is rendered.
Ideal for updating terminal window titles or setting dynamic status indicators.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
precmd() {
    print -n "\\033]0;${PWD}\\007"
}
.Ed
.It Ic preexec
Executed immediately after a command is entered and before execution begins.
The full command string is passed as the first parameter
.Pq Va $1 .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
preexec() {
    echo "[executing: $1]"
}
.Ed
.It Ic chpwd
Executed automatically whenever the current working directory changes via
.Ic cd ,
.Ic pushd ,
or
.Ic popd .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
chpwd() {
    ls -la
}
.Ed
.El
.Sh DIRECTORY STACK AND NAVIGATION
In addition to standard directory changes,
.Nm
maintains an in-memory directory history stack:
.Bl -tag -width "pushd"
.It Ic cd Op Fl L | Fl P Op Ar dir
Changes the current working directory to
.Ar dir .
If
.Ar dir
is omitted, defaults to the user's home directory.
If
.Ar dir
is
.Ql - ,
switches to
.Va $OLDPWD .
Respects the colon-separated search path defined in
.Va $CDPATH .
.It Ic pushd Op Ar dir | +N | -N Op Fl n
Pushes the target directory onto the directory stack
.Va DIRSTACK ,
or rotates stack entries.
.It Ic popd Op +N | -N Op Fl n
Removes entries from the top or specified offset of the directory stack.
.It Ic dirs Op Fl c | Fl l | Fl p | Fl v
Displays the contents of the directory stack.
With
.Fl v ,
displays entries numbered on separate lines.
.It Va DIRSTACK
An indexed array containing the current directory stack, with
.Va ${DIRSTACK[0]}
representing the active working directory.
.El
.Sh COMMAND HISTORY AND MULTI-SESSION SYNCHRONIZATION
.Nm
manages command history with instant synchronization:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy Real-Time Persistence:
Executed commands are immediately appended to
.Pa ~/.zbash_history ,
making command history instantaneously available to concurrent terminal windows and tabs.
.It
.Sy Duplicate Suppression:
Unique history tracking prevents repetitive consecutive commands from cluttering history storage.
.It
.Sy History Control Variables:
.Bl -tag -width "HISTCONTROL"
.It Va HISTSIZE
Maximum number of command lines retained in session memory (default: 2000).
.It Va HISTFILE
Path to the persistent history file.
.It Va HISTCONTROL
Colon-separated flags such as
.Ql ignoredups
(ignore duplicate lines) and
.Ql ignorespace
(ignore lines starting with whitespace).
.It Va HISTIGNORE
Colon-separated glob patterns defining commands to exclude from history recording.
.El
.El
.Sh PARAMETERS AND ARRAYS
.Nm
supports standard shell variables, indexed arrays, and associative hash maps:
.Ss Indexed Arrays
Indexed arrays are zero-based
.Pq Va ${arr[0]} :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fruits=(apple banana cherry)
echo "${fruits[0]}"       # Outputs: apple
echo "${fruits[@]}"       # Outputs all elements
echo "${#fruits[@]}"      # Outputs array length (3)
.Ed
.Ss Associative Arrays
Associative arrays (hash maps) are declared using
.Ic declare -A
or
.Ic typeset -A :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
declare -A user_roles
user_roles[alice]="admin"
user_roles[bob]="engineer"
echo "${user_roles[alice]}"  # Outputs: admin
.Ed
.Ss Special Parameters
.Bl -tag -width "$?"
.It Va $?
Exit status of the most recently executed foreground pipeline.
.It Va $#
Number of positional parameters passed to the current shell or function.
.It Va $$
Process ID of the active shell instance.
.It Va $!
Process ID of the most recently started background job.
.It Va $0
Name of the shell or script currently executing.
.It Va $@ , $*
Expands to the positional parameters.
When double-quoted,
.Ql "$@"
expands each parameter as a distinct word.
.El
.Sh BUILTIN COMMANDS
.Nm
includes a comprehensive suite of high-performance builtins:
.Bl -tag -width "readonly"
.It Ic alias Op Ar name[=value] ...
Defines or prints command aliases.
.It Ic builtin Ar builtin-name Op Ar arg ...
Executes a shell builtin directly, bypassing shell functions with the same name.
.It Ic caller Op Ar expr
Returns the context of any active subroutine call in the execution frame stack.
.It Ic cd Op Fl L | Fl P Op Ar dir
Changes working directory.
.It Ic command Op Fl p Op Fl v | Fl V Ar command Op Ar arg ...
Runs a command bypassing shell function lookups.
.It Ic declare Op Fl a | Fl A | Fl i | Fl l | Fl r | Fl x | Fl p Op Ar name[=val] ...
Declares shell variables, attributes, indexed arrays, or associative hash maps.
.It Ic dirs Op Fl c | Fl l | Fl p | Fl v
Displays directory stack entries.
.It Ic disown Op Fl h | Fl a | Fl r Op Ar jobspec ...
Removes jobs from active job table management.
.It Ic echo Op Fl n | Fl e | Fl E Op Ar string ...
Outputs strings to standard output with optional escape sequence interpretation.
.It Ic eval Op Ar arg ...
Concatenates arguments and evaluates them as a shell command.
.It Ic exec Op Fl cl Op Fl a Ar name Op Ar command Op Ar arg ...
Replaces the active shell process with the specified command.
.It Ic exit Op Ar n
Terminates the shell with exit code
.Ar n .
.It Ic export Op Fl n | Fl f | Fl p Op Ar name[=val] ...
Marks variables for automatic export to the environments of child processes.
.It Ic fc Op Fl e Ar ename Op Fl nlr Op Ar first Op Ar last
Selects and re-executes or edits commands from the history list.
.It Ic fg Op Ar jobspec , Ic bg Op Ar jobspec ...
Moves jobs between foreground and background execution.
.It Ic help Op Fl s Op Ar pattern
Displays native contextual documentation and synopses for builtin commands.
.It Ic history Op Fl c | Fl d Ar offset | Fl awrn Op Ar filename
Displays or manipulates the active command history list.
.It Ic jobs Op Fl l | Fl p | Fl n | Fl r | Fl s Op Ar jobspec ...
Lists active background jobs and their state.
.It Ic kill Op Fl s Ar sigspec | Fl n Ar signum | Fl Ar sigspec Ar pid | jobspec ...
Sends signals to processes or background jobs.
.It Ic let Ar expr Op Ar expr ...
Evaluates arithmetic expressions.
.It Ic local Op Ar name[=val] ...
Declares variables scoped locally within the active function frame.
.It Ic popd Op +N | -N Op Fl n
Removes entries from the directory stack.
.It Ic printf Ar format Op Ar arguments ...
Formats and prints arguments according to format specification strings.
.It Ic pushd Op Ar dir | +N | -N Op Fl n
Pushes a directory onto the directory stack.
.It Ic pwd Op Fl L | Fl P
Prints the absolute path of the current working directory.
.It Ic read Op Fl r Op Fl p Ar prompt Op Fl t Ar timeout Op Fl a Ar array Op Fl n Ar nchars Op Fl s Op Ar name ...
Reads a line from standard input into shell variables.
.It Ic readonly Op Fl p | Fl a | Fl A Op Ar name[=val] ...
Marks variables or arrays as read-only.
.It Ic return Op Ar n
Exits a shell function with return status
.Ar n .
.It Ic set Op Fl e | Fl u | Fl x | Fl v | Fl o Ar option Op Fl \- Ar arg ...
Configures shell environment options and positional parameters.
.It Ic shift Op Ar n
Shifts positional parameters left by
.Ar n
positions.
.It Ic shopt Op Fl s | Fl u Op Fl p | Fl q Op Ar optname ...
Toggles optional shell behavior settings.
.It Ic source Ar filename Op Ar arguments ...
Reads and executes commands from
.Ar filename
in the current shell context.
.It Ic test Ar expr
.It \fB[\fR Ar expr \fB]\fR
Evaluates conditional expressions.
.It Ic [[ Ar expression ]]
Evaluates advanced conditional expressions with pattern comparison.
.It Ic trap Op Fl l | Fl p Op Ar action Ar sigspec ...
Defines signal handlers and termination traps.
.It Ic typeset
Identical to
.Ic declare .
.It Ic unalias Op Fl a Op Ar name ...
Removes command aliases.
.It Ic unset Op Fl v | Fl f Op Ar name ...
Unsets shell variables or functions.
.It Ic wait Op Ar pid | jobspec ...
Waits for background processes or jobs to terminate and returns their exit code.
.It Ic zedit Op Fl t | Fl g Op Fl h Op Ar filename
Opens the integrated dual-surface terminal micro-IDE for file editing and execution.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "ZBASH_CMD_DURATION"
.It Va CDPATH
The search path for the
.Ic cd
builtin.
.It Va HOME
The default directory for the
.Ic cd
builtin.
.It Va OLDPWD
The previous working directory set by the
.Ic cd
builtin.
.It Va PATH
The search path for executable programs.
.It Va PWD
The current working directory.
.It Va ZBASH_CMD_DURATION
The duration of the last foreground command in milliseconds.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/zprofile"
.It Pa /etc/zprofile
Global system initialization file for login shells.
.It Pa ~/.zprofile
User initialization file for login shells.
.It Pa ~/.zbashrc
User initialization file for interactive shells.
.It Pa ~/.zbash_history
Default persistent history file.
.El
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
complies with POSIX.1-2017 (IEEE Std 1003.1-2017) shell language specifications
while providing high-velocity silicon optimizations and modern interactive developer ergonomics.
